Here's an ugly suggestion for the monster confirmation question:

Leave it up to the user via something like:
CONFIRM = "eGh@"
with the default being CONFIRM="".

Then, in the above example, any attack on an e,G,h or @ is confirmed, whether
the monster is peaceful or not.  It doesn't give things away, since the
player has to know what to specify (ie what may be peaceful), and it doesn't
single out peaceful monsters in any case.  Of course it would be a pain
since many unfriendly monsters would also be confirmed, but maybe that's the
price to pay for safety.

>attacking peaceful monsters should be something you could do at your
>discretion if somehow you could discriminate peaceful monsters from
>other nasties, no questions asked.

Yes, and how to discriminate is the real problem.

>a blessed potion of invisibility should unconditionally make you
>intrinsically invisible, no questions asked....

I agree completely.  This seemed more like a (misplaced) feature than a
confirmation, though.

>cruising aimlessly around a treacherous dungeon and falling into a
>potentially drowning pool of water should be a problem! you shouldn't
>be able to prevent this by saying "i never want to fall into pools
>and so if i attempt to then please warn me and give me an oppurtunity
>to not make a mistake that i'll regret". the strategy involved is that
>you must, at this point, be on your toes (i.e. pay attention, take care
>and be deliberate).

I disagree here.  I lost my best game coming back up on the castle level
with the Amulet when I fell into the moat.  I have no idea how it
happened.  I was fighting a succubus one second, and the next one I
had drowned.  Since there are benefits to swimming in pools, it
doesn't seem like such a problem to me to confirm it.  If you're really
worried about giving too much away, you could also confirm other rare 
things to compensate, like

Really pick up the Amulet of Yendor [yn]?

There are some things in nethack that become tedious on a slow machine.
It's also not always clear what's happening.  For instance, <shift> move
on my AT can take a long time, and it's often not clear if I mistyped,
or if the machine is thinking.  This is espcially true on crowed levels 
(such as the castle level).  If you wait, then hit another key, and it
turns out the machine was thinking, it's very easy to do things you didn't
really want to.  Perhaps some kind of prompt could be added, like a >
in the upper left hand corner to signal "Yes I'm waiting for you to hit
a key"?

Well, I have more pet peeves, but I'll just say that I don't think the
player should be punished for limitations of the interface/speed of
hardware (no slur against the dev team implied).

